 ever really understand the  experiences of others kai andy go for it  um hi good evening um  it depends where you are but hello  anyway and i think the key part of this  is really to i want to focus on the  power aspect of this um because language  is interesting but really this is the  whole issue here is the power and we we  can't we're not having this conversation  in a vacuum right  um can you understand the experiences i  guess in some theoretical realm  potentially we have to understand um  that you know there isn't just this  marketplace of ideas where everybody  comes to this equally we actually we're  in a place um an arena the places that  produce ideas and language and where  people can speak a deeply problematic  rooted in certainties of racism um and  deeply imbalanced right so when we're  even starting to have that conversation  um it is accomplished you have to really  understand the long history of the real  question here is who gets to speak who  gets to write um and that really is the  knob of this debate right for far too  long if you think about the ways that  you like britain etc um black and brown  creators have really been locked out  from that process so you haven't had as  many black and brown voices there in the  first place and not just locked out  accidentally locked out on purpose right  this is one of the ways  the racism works so we're not there  right i'll give you a concrete i said  and we like to think this has changed uh  dramatically but it really hasn't right  so if you think about publishing  industry today if you think about who is  the producers and as somebody who works  you know really does work a lot with  mainstream producers mainstream  publishers is you'll be very surprised  how wide it is right so there's all the  the voices you're making the decisions  are still still very still very um  narrow  um actually the book i wrote before this  book was called that black retailing  black radicalism from the 21st century  and 